ashley herrmann first noticed he had  trouble walking in 2013.  a year later he was diagnosed with  progressive multiple sclerosis  and doctors warned him it will be a  matter of time before he needed a  wheelchair  it's got progressively worse over the  last five  six years in that i now have  trouble with my right hand side of my  body  moving my arm and moving my leg  to the point where i can't really go for  long walks i can walk with a stick  but i do have a distinctive  limp as i walk there is currently  nothing that can slow down progressive  ms  but a brand new clinical trial is  promising to change that  called octopus scientists say it could  develop new options for treatment up to  three times faster  octopus is revolutionary it's going to  speed up the development of those much  needed  life life-changing new treatments for  people with progressive forms of ms  that currently have no treatments that  can stop their ms getting worse  so we'll be able to test new treatments  more quickly by testing them together  but also by combining trial phases it  will be much quicker much more efficient  for the scientists but also for people  involved in the trial  and we do believe it will stop ms don't  stop thinking about tomorrow  don't stop the illness attacks the  nerves in the body  it's painful and disabling but this  trial which hopes to recruit volunteers  later this year  has given hope to some who suffer from  it  i've got young children i want to still  be as active  as i can for them so that i'm not  confined to a wheelchair  i'm not stuck in doors all day every day  i can still have  as reason as reasonably an active life  as possible and for many like ashley  it's that hope  that keeps them going milena  vaselinovich  sky news
 

